      "Welcome to Spanish corporate and compliance law. Spanish corporate law centers on the Ley de Sociedades de Capital, the LSC, and its most distinctive recent feature is a 2010 reform to Article 31 bis of the Spanish Criminal Code introducing genuine corporate criminal liability, a company itself, not just its individual officers, can now be criminally prosecuted, with an adequate compliance program, a modelo de organización y gestión, covering risk assessment, control measures, and a reporting channel, serving as a real legal defense if implemented and followed before the offense. Over the next forty minutes you will work through ten ideas in the order a real Spanish corporate governance question actually unfolds. We start with the foundational choice of company form every Spanish business makes at formation, the shareholders' own formal channel of power, and the single-tier board structure Spanish companies actually use. We then turn to the standard directors are held to, the disclosure regime governing deals with a company's own insiders, and a genuinely distinctive form of personal liability Spanish directors face when a company should have been dissolved but was not. We cover the corporate criminal liability regime itself and exactly what an adequate compliance program actually has to contain to serve as a real defense against it. We close with the securities regulator overseeing listed companies and the comply-or-explain governance code that shapes their practice without ever technically binding them. Keep one comparison running underneath everything you hear: where Germany mandates a structure and France offers a genuine choice, ask which of the two Spain has actually followed, and where neither neighbor goes, ask what Spain built on its own.",
