    connections:
      "Ten ideas, one recurring comparison: where Spanish criminal law theory borrowed its analytical structure from Germany, ask whether its actual criminal procedure followed Germany, followed France, or built something genuinely its own. Tipicidad, antijuridicidad, and culpabilidad is the German-influenced analytical backbone for any offense, and el principio de legalidad places Spain closer to Germany's mandatory-prosecution default than to France's own broader discretion. Prisión provisional and the juez de instrucción govern how a suspect is held and investigated before trial, the latter a structural link to France rather than Germany, and la conformidad resolves many cases through a negotiated, judge-supervised resolution considerably closer to American practice than either neighbor's own narrower tools. The Tribunal del Jurado handles trial for the specific serious crimes on its own statutory list, with guilt and sentencing structurally separated between jury and judge, and acusación particular and acción popular give the victim, and in Spain's genuinely distinctive case any citizen at all, a real prosecutorial role beyond the Ministerio Fiscal. Presunción de inocencia protects the accused throughout this entire process with its own robust constitutional enforcement pathway, responsabilidad penal corporativa extends the whole framework to companies as defendants in their own right, and recursos, apelación and casación, finally, determine what, if anything, can still be challenged once a case concludes. As you read any Spanish criminal case from here forward, try running it through all ten ideas in order, and resist the reflex to assume Spain's German-derived theory means its actual procedure follows Germany at every turn.",
